---
/*
  Terms.

  Written to match the site people can use today. Historical data belongs on the privacy
  page rather than in the rules for reading this one.

  Not written by a lawyer. See the note at the foot, which is deliberately on the page
  rather than only in the source.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';

const lastChanged = new Date(SITE.termsUpdated).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'short',
  year: 'numeric'
});
---

<Base
  title="Terms"
  description="Who owns the work here, how you may use it, and where your judgment still matters."
>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="shell read">
      <aside class="rail">
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Who runs this</dt><dd><a href="/about/">Michael Jolley</a></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Site content</dt><dd>Mine unless credited</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Source code</dt><dd>MIT licensed</dd></div>
          <div><dt>The rules</dt><dd><a href="/conduct/">Code of conduct</a></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Last changed</dt><dd>{lastChanged}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <article data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-filter="kind:page">
        <header class="storyhead">
          <span class="path">terms</span>
          <h1>
            Use what helps.
            <span class="squiggle"><span>Make your own call.</span></span>
          </h1>
          <p class="lede">
            This is a personal site run by one person. There is no company behind it, no
            support desk, and no fine print designed to catch you out. These are the rules I
            would explain out loud.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="prose terms-body">
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="tag">The short version</span>
            <p>
              The work here is shared to help you think and build. Read it, link to it, and
              quote a short passage with credit. Ask before republishing the whole thing.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2>Who owns what</h2>
          <p>
            Unless a page credits somebody else, the articles, videos, images, transcripts,
            and site copy are mine. Work credited to somebody else stays theirs.
          </p>
          <p>
            The source code for this site is available under the MIT License. That license
            covers the software. It does not turn the published content into open source
            material.
          </p>

          <h2>Using the work</h2>
          <p>
            Sharing a link is always welcome. Quoting a short section with a clear credit and
            link back is fine too. If you want to republish a full article, transcript, image,
            or collection, ask first. The answer is often yes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not use my name, face, logo, or work to suggest I endorse a product, service,
            or opinion unless I actually said I do.
          </p>

          <h2>Accuracy and advice</h2>
          <p>
            I try to make the work practical and accurate, but software changes and I get
            things wrong. Check versions, read the official documentation, and test the idea
            somewhere safe before it gets near production.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nothing here creates a client, employment, financial, legal, or other professional
            relationship. It is experience shared in public, not a substitute for advice from
            somebody who knows your situation.
          </p>

          <h2>Where this site sends you</h2>
          <p>
            Links out are links out. Once you are on YouTube, Twitch, GitHub or anywhere else,
            their terms apply and mine have stopped.
          </p>

          <h2>The part every site has</h2>
          <p>
            The site is provided as it is. I run it carefully, but I am one person, and it can
            be down, wrong, or briefly very strange. Use what helps, verify what matters, and
            make your own call.
          </p>
          <p>
            If something here causes you a loss, I am sorry, and I cannot be liable for it,
            to the extent the law where you live lets me say that. Where it does not, the
            law wins.
          </p>

          <h2>Privacy</h2>
          <p>
            The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> explains what the site stores, why it is
            stored, and how to ask for it to be removed.
          </p>

          <h2>Changing these terms</h2>
          <p>
            The date is in the sidebar and it only moves when something actually changed. A
            change applies from that date forward.
          </p>

          <div class="callout">
            <span class="tag">Not a lawyer</span>
            <p>
              I wrote this myself, in plain English, because a page nobody can read protects
              nobody. It has not been through a solicitor yet. If you are reading it as a
              lawyer and something here is unenforceable or missing, tell me and I will fix
              it rather than defend it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-actions terms-acts">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/articles/">Read the articles</a>
          <a class="btn" href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .terms-body {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .terms-acts {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
